# UI Consistency & Component Reuse

## Core Principles

1. **Reuse existing components first** - Always check `src/components/ui/` and `src/components/features/` before writing new UI code.

2. **Use shadcn/ui components** - The project uses shadcn/ui. Always prefer:
   - `Button` from `@/components/ui/button`
   - `Dialog`, `AlertDialog` from `@/components/ui/dialog`
   - `Input`, `Label` from `@/components/ui/input`
   - `DropdownMenu` from `@/components/ui/dropdown-menu`
   - `Select` from `@/components/ui/select`
   - `Toast` via `sonner`
   - Existing skeleton components in `src/components/ui/skeletons/`

3. **Do not create custom modals, dialogs, or alerts** - Use `AlertDialog` or `Dialog` components instead of raw HTML/CSS overlays. See `AlertDialog` usage in `ProjectDocumentsManager.tsx` as reference.

4. **Consistent styling** - Use Tailwind classes that match the project's design system:
   - Colors: `text-[#101828]`, `text-gray-500`, `bg-white`, `border-gray-200`
   - Spacing: `px-4 py-2`, `space-y-6`
   - Typography: `text-sm font-medium`, `text-[24px]`

5. **Never write inline modal/dialog HTML** - Always wrap shadcn/ui components. The project already has all necessary dialog primitives.

6. **Check before adding icons** - Use `lucide-react` icons which are already imported across the project.

## Anti-patterns

```tsx
// ❌ BAD - Custom alert overlay
<div className="fixed inset-0 bg-black/40">
  <div className="bg-white p-4">Confirm?</div>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD - Reuse AlertDialog
import { AlertDialog, AlertDialogAction, AlertDialogCancel, AlertDialogContent, AlertDialogDescription, AlertDialogFooter, AlertDialogHeader, AlertDialogTitle } from '@/components/ui/alert-dialog';
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD - Custom button
<button className="px-4 py-2 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded-lg">Submit</button>

// ✅ GOOD - Reuse Button component
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
<Button>Submit</Button>
```
